# Release trains (required)

Classify the change first, then branch off that train. See
[CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) **Release trains**. **v0.6.0** is
tagged; Unreleased on `main` is 0.6.1.

| Train | Base branch | What lands |
|---|---|---|
| **0.6.1** (patch) | `main` | Fixes, small refactors, improvements, dependency bumps, tests for existing behavior. No new user-facing capability, config keys, models, or I/O `kind`s. |
| **0.7.0** (minor) | `release/0.7.0` | New features and new public surface. PRs target this branch, not `main`. |

If ambiguous, put it on **0.6.1** unless it adds user-facing capability.
Never land 0.7.0 features on `main` until v0.6.1 ships. After that tag,
`main` is 0.7.0 and new features land on `main`.

## Changelog

`## [Unreleased]` is per-branch: next patch (0.6.1) on `main`, 0.7.0 on
`release/0.7.0`. Dated sections stay frozen.

## After v0.6.1

Cherry-pick each 0.6.1 fix from `main` onto `release/0.7.0`
(`git cherry-pick` the commit that landed the change on `main`). On
conflict, keep 0.7.0 feature code and re-apply the fix; if it still will
not apply, abort and PR a port to `release/0.7.0` — do not change `main`
to match 0.7.0-only APIs.
Hotfixes that must ship on both trains land on `main` first, then
cherry-pick immediately — do not open them only against `release/0.7.0`.
Do not merge 0.7.0 into `main` before 0.6.1 ships. After the tag, merge
`release/0.7.0` into `main`; `main` becomes the 0.7.0 train. Stop
cherry-picking onto `release/0.7.0`.

When merging `release/0.7.0` into `main`, prefer `main` for 0.6.1
fixes on a shared surface and `release/0.7.0` for 0.7.0-only APIs
(see CONTRIBUTING **Cherry-picks and merge-backs**). If `main`
diverged, cherry-pick remaining 0.6.1 commits onto `release/0.7.0`
before the merge; do not force-push `main`. Tag from `main` after that
merge (0.6.1 tags the completing commit on `main` directly).

Version bump still happens on the PR that completes that version.
